        \[
\text{All faces are congruent }30^{\circ}\!-\!75^{\circ}\!-\!75^{\circ}\text{ triangles.}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 1.  What can happen at a vertex ?

A vertex of the polyhedron is incident with some number of faces.
If a face meets the vertex at its \(30^{\circ}\)–angle we call the
vertex a **\(30^{\circ}\)‑vertex**; if it meets at a \(75^{\circ}\)–angle we
call it a **\(75^{\circ}\)‑vertex**.

*If \(a\) faces meet at a \(30^{\circ}\)‑vertex then the sum of the face
angles there is \(a\cdot30^{\circ}<360^{\circ}\); hence  

\[
3\le a\le 11 .
\tag{1}
\]

*If \(b\) faces meet at a \(75^{\circ}\)‑vertex then  

\[
b\cdot75^{\circ}<360^{\circ}\Longrightarrow b\in\{3,4\}.
\tag{2}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 2.  Counting incidences

Let  

* \(V_{30}\) = number of \(30^{\circ}\)‑vertices,
* \(V_{75}\) = number of \(75^{\circ}\)‑vertices,
* \(F=m\)   = number of triangular faces,
* \(E\)      = number of edges.

Each face contributes one \(30^{\circ}\)‑corner and two \(75^{\circ}\)‑corners, so  

\[
m = aV_{30},\qquad 2m = bV_{75}.
\tag{3}
\]

Every face has three edges and each edge belongs to two faces, hence  

\[
3m = 2E\quad\Longrightarrow\quad E=\frac{3m}{2},
\tag{4}
\]

so \(m\) must be even.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 3.  Euler’s formula

For a convex polyhedron \(V-E+F=2\) where \(V=V_{30}+V_{75}\).  
Using (3)–(4),

\[
\frac{m}{a}+\frac{2m}{b}-\frac{3m}{2}+m = 2 .
\]

After simplification

\[
m\Bigl(\frac1a+\frac2b-\frac12\Bigr)=2,
\qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
m=\frac{2}{\displaystyle\frac1a+\frac2b-\frac12 } .
\tag{5}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 4.  The combinatorial bound

Insert the only possible values (1)–(2) into (5).  
The integer values of \(m\) obtained are  

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
a & b & m \\ \hline
3 & 3 & 4\\
3 & 4 & 6\\
4 & 4 & 8\\
5 & 4 &10\\
6 & 3 & 6\\
6 & 4 &12\\
7 & 4 &14\\
8 & 4 &16\\
9 & 4 &18\\
10& 4 &20\\
11& 4 &22
\end{array}
\]

Thus any convex polyhedron with the required faces satisfies  

\[
\boxed{m\le 22 } .
\tag{6}
\]

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 5.  Realising the bound

Take a regular \(n\)-gon (\(n\ge 3\)) as a base and erect two apices
above and below its plane, forming an **\(n\)-gonal bipyramid**.
The polyhedron has  

\[
F = 2n\quad\text{triangular faces,}
\qquad
\text{one apex vertex of degree }n,
\qquad
\text{each base vertex of degree }4 .
\]

Hence for the bipyramid we have \(a=n\) and \(b=4\); formula (5) gives
\(m=2n\), exactly the number of faces of the bipyramid.

Choose the height of the apices so that the triangle formed by an apex
and an edge of the base has apex angle \(30^{\circ}\).  
If the side length of the base edge is \(s\) and the equal sides of the
triangle are \(L\), the law of cosines gives  

\[
\cos 30^{\circ}=1-\frac{s^{2}}{2L^{2}}
\Longrightarrow\frac{L}{s}= \frac{1}{\sqrt{2-\sqrt3}} .
\]

For any \(n\) we can pick the radius of the regular \(n\)-gon and then a
height \(h>0\) so that the distance from an apex to a base vertex equals
\(L\); the required height exists because \(L\) can be made arbitrarily
large by increasing \(h\).  Consequently **every bipyramid** can be made
with congruent \(30^{\circ}\!-\!75^{\circ}\!-\!75^{\circ}\) triangles.

Taking \(n=11\) we obtain a bipyramid with  

\[
m = 2\cdot 11 = 22
\]

faces, and at the two apices \(a=11\) faces meet (each contributes a
\(30^{\circ}\) angle), while at each of the eleven base vertices
\(b=4\) faces meet (each contributes a \(75^{\circ}\) angle).  This
polyhedron satisfies all conditions, so the bound (6) is sharp.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
### 6.  Answer

The maximum possible number of faces is  

\[
\boxed{\,m_{\max}=22\,}.
\]

It is attained by the regular 11‑gonal bipyramid whose faces are
congruent triangles with angles \(30^{\circ},75^{\circ},75^{\circ}\).

